<h2 id="correction"><span class="secno">§1</span>First, a correction — the stack-map story</h2>

<p>You wrote: <em>“Fortress emits class file version 50 (V1_6) so that it can omit emitting the stack maps, forcing the JVM to use the old inference verifier.”</em> That was my earlier framing, and the scouts proved it wrong in an interesting way. The truth is better.</p>

<div class="callout">
<span class="callout-label">What actually happens</span>
<p>Every ClassWriter in the entire system — compile-time codegen <em>and</em> the runtime loader — is constructed with <code>COMPUTE_FRAMES</code>, which means ASM computes and emits stack map frames for every class, always. Fortress never omits them. The convention is so total that codegen passes a joke constant, <code>Naming.ignoredMaxsParameter</code>, to <code>visitMaxs</code> because ASM recomputes those too.</p>
</div>

<p>So why pin the classfile version to V1_6? The single source of truth is <code>InstantiatingClassloader.java:78</code> — <code>JVM_BYTECODE_VERSION = Opcodes.V1_6</code> — used by both halves of the system. V1_6 classfiles get <em>lenient</em> treatment from the verifier: frames are advisory, and the JVM falls back to the old inference verifier when they disagree. From V1_7 (strictly: 50 is the last version where failover is permitted) frames become mandatory and load-bearing. Fortress computes frames but doesn’t want to be <em>graded</em> on them — because of one soft spot, which is the real story and lives in <code>ManglingClassWriter</code> (§4): when ASM needs the common supertype of two Fortress types to compute a frame, Fortress shrugs and says <code>java/lang/Object</code>. Under V1_6 rules that white lie is harmless. Under strict verification it might not be. <strong>That</strong> is why raising the emitted classfile version is bundled with future compiler work rather than being a build-flag flip.</p>

<h2 id="bigpicture"><span class="secno">§2</span>The big picture: a compiler that mails work to the future</h2>

<p>The load-bearing design decision of the whole Fortress backend is this: <strong>the compiler never generates code for an instantiated generic.</strong> When it compiles <code>List⟦ZZ32⟧</code>, it does not emit a class for it. It emits two other things instead:</p>

<ol>
<li>A <strong>template class</strong> — a real .class file whose internal name still contains the <em>formal</em> type parameters, e.g. <code>List⟦T⟧</code>, stored in the component’s jar under a stem name with empty Oxford brackets (<code>List⟦⟧</code>). A comment in <code>CodeGen.java:2205</code> says it plainly: <em>“the class is not really a class; it is a template to be filled in.”</em></li>
<li>At every <em>use</em> site, a plain JVM reference — <code>NEW</code>, <code>GETSTATIC</code>, <code>INVOKE…</code> — against a class name that does not exist on disk: <code>List⟦ZZ32⟧</code>, spelled out with real Unicode Oxford brackets in the constant pool.</li>
</ol>

<p>The trick works because loading is lazy and programmable. When the JVM first touches <code>List⟦ZZ32⟧</code>, it asks the classloader, and Fortress’s <code>InstantiatingClassloader</code> reads the request name, recognizes the brackets, fetches the template plus a sidecar file describing its parameters, substitutes <code>ZZ32</code> for <code>T</code> through every instruction of every method, and hands the JVM a freshly written class that never existed until that moment. Generics are reified — each instantiation is a real, distinct JVM class with real types in its signatures — and the combinatorial explosion is paid for strictly on demand.</p>

<div class="pipeline">
  <p class="pipe-band">Compile time — <code style="background:none;border:none">fortress compile</code></p>
  <div class="pnode"><span class="pname">hello.fss</span><span class="pdesc">parse (Rats! PEG) → disambiguate → typecheck (Scala) → desugar → overload rewrite</span></div>
  <p class="parrow">↓ <small>typed AST</small></p>
