</div>

<p>Note the symmetry: <code>ManglingClassWriter</code> appears at the bottom of <em>both</em> halves. It is the one funnel every Fortress class passes through on its way to becoming real bytes, whether written at compile time or conjured at load time. That’s why it deserved its own explainer, and why it gets §4.</p>

<h2 id="names"><span class="secno">§3</span>Names as a wire format</h2>

<p>If the loader must build classes from their names, the names must carry data. Fortress class names are not identifiers; they are a serialization format with two layers.</p>

<h3>Layer 1 — semantic tags</h3>

<p>A set of Unicode characters, deliberately chosen from the Miscellaneous Symbols and Dingbats blocks (the design note in the papers: they <em>“should not be mathematical operators, nor letters”</em> — nothing a Fortress program could itself use), each encoding one fact:</p>

<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Name in source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">✉</td><td><code>ENVELOPE</code></td><td>a closure — a first-class reference to a top-level function; the loader synthesizes the wrapper class on sight</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">⚙</td><td><code>GEAR</code></td><td>a generic function; checked <em>before</em> the closure test, routes to template expansion</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">⟦ ⟧</td><td>Oxford brackets</td><td>generic type parameters — empty in template file names, filled at use sites</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">❮ ❯</td><td>heavy angle brackets</td><td>operator (<code>opr</code>) static parameters, e.g. <code>BinOp❮PLUS❯$RTTIc</code></td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">☃</td><td><code>SNOWMAN</code></td><td>the void type <code>()</code> as a static parameter. The comment beside it in <code>Naming.java</code>: “sigh.”</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">☞</td><td><code>INDEX</code></td><td>functional-method trampoline: <code>f☞2</code> = “self was parameter 2”</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">☝</td><td><code>UP_INDEX</code></td><td>generic-method template marker</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">✚ ✖</td><td>heavy plus / ✕</td><td>“stop rewriting here” — blocks further substitution during generic expansion</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">♙</td><td><code>NON_OVERLOADED_TAG</code></td><td>white chess pawn, appended when an overload dispatcher steals a function’s canonical name</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">☕</td><td><code>FOREIGN_TAG</code></td><td>marks names imported from Java. Yes: Java is tagged with a cup of coffee.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="glyphcell">⎆</td><td><code>ENTER</code></td><td>RPN-encoded <code>nat</code> arithmetic in names, evaluated at instantiation time</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>A single real name, from the papers — the closure class for a top-level <code>printlnZZ(n:ZZ32):()</code> in component <code>C6</code>:</p>

<pre><code>C6$printlnZZ✉$Arrow⟦com/sun/fortress/compiler/runtimeValues/FZZ32,☃⟧</code></pre>

<p>Everything the loader needs is in there: which component, which function, that it’s a closure (✉), and the full arrow type — domain <code>FZZ32</code>, range void (☃). The loader parses this and emits a forwarding class with no template involved.</p>

<h3>Layer 2 — “symbolic freedom” escaping</h3>

<p>The JVM forbids <code>/ . ; $ &lt; &gt; [ ]</code> and a few others in names. Fortress escapes them using John Rose’s <em>symbolic freedom</em> encoding (the same scheme designed for JSR-292/invokedynamic): <code>/</code>→<code>\|</code>, <code>.</code>→<code>\,</code>, <code>;</code>→<code>\?</code>, <code>$</code>→<code>\%</code>, <code>&lt;</code>→<code>\^</code>, <code>&gt;</code>→<code>\_</code>, <code>[</code>→<code>\{</code>, <code>]</code>→<code>\}</code>, <code>:</code>→<code>\!</code>, with a <code>\=</code> prefix marking a mangled name. Crucially, the Oxford brackets are <em>not</em> escaped — they are legal to the JVM and must survive intact so the loader can re-parse the structure at load time. This layering is exactly what <code>ManglingClassWriter</code> applies.</p>

<h2 id="mcw"><span class="secno">§4</span>ManglingClassWriter — the funnel</h2>
<p class="cite">ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/codegen/ManglingClassWriter.java (172 lines)</p>

<p>The file you asked about is small and does exactly three jobs:</p>

<p><strong>Job 1: mangle every name on the way in.</strong> It sits in front of an ASM <code>ClassWriter</code> and rewrites class names, superclass names, interface lists, field and method names, descriptors, and signatures through <code>Naming.mangleFortressIdentifier</code> / <code>mangleMethodSignature</code> / <code>mangleMemberName</code> — layer 2 of §3. Codegen upstream gets to think in clean Fortress names; only at this choke point do they become JVM-legal. A wrinkle: with <code>TWEAK_ERASED_UNIONS = true</code>, the erased content of union types (§7 of the TypeMapping paper) is appended to member names so that two methods differing only in erased union types don’t collide.</p>

<p><strong>Job 2: answer ASM’s type questions — badly, on purpose.</strong> Because everything uses <code>COMPUTE_FRAMES</code>, ASM must sometimes compute the common supertype of two types to build a stack map frame. ASM’s default does this by <em>loading both classes with Class.forName</em> — which for Fortress types would recursively re-enter the InstantiatingClassloader mid-write, and for template names would ask for classes that can’t exist. The inner <code>FortressClassWriter</code> handles this with a Sun-original cop-out, comment preserved verbatim:</p>

<pre><code>protected String getCommonSuperClass(String type1, String type2) {
    try {
        return super.getCommonSuperClass(type1, type2);
    } catch (Throwable e) {
        // This returns java.lang.Object as the CommonSuperClass of
        // anything involving an interface type.  Since all Fortress
        // traits correspond to interface types, we ought to be able
        // to do the same.
        //
        // That said, it still feels wrong.
        return Naming.javaObject;
    }
}</code></pre>
<p class="cite">ManglingClassWriter.java:39–57 — “That said, it still feels wrong” is Sun’s comment, not ours</p>

<p>This is sound-ish because Fortress traits compile to interfaces and the pre-V1_7 verifier doesn’t strictly check interface receiver types — the frames only need to be plausible, not perfect. It is the direct reason the V1_6 pin exists (§1).</p>

<p><strong>Job 3: debug taps.</strong> <code>-Dfortress.bytecode.list=true</code> wraps every method in ASM’s <code>TraceMethodVisitor</code> for a disassembly listing; a sibling property <code>fortress.bytecodes.expanded.directory</code> (checked in the loader) writes every runtime-expanded class into an <code>expanded.jar</code> you can open with <code>javap</code>. These two switches are the archaeology toolkit for everything in this report.</p>

<p>One historical note that is <em>ours</em>, not Sun’s: under ASM 3 this class simply subclassed <code>ClassWriter</code> and overrode its visit methods. ASM 4 made those methods final, so the class was restructured on our ASM 9 rung into a <code>ClassVisitor</code> delegating to an internal writer — same behavior, modern shape. The comment at the top of the file (lines 23–27) documenting this is a revival-era edit.</p>

<h2 id="icl"><span class="secno">§5</span>InstantiatingClassloader — the loader that writes classes</h2>
<p class="cite">ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/runtimeSystem/InstantiatingClassloader.java (2,946 lines)</p>

<p>The runtime half. A singleton (<code>ONLY</code>), installed as the classloader for all Fortress code, that overrides <code>loadClass</code> itself — it must intercept <em>every</em> request, not just cache misses. Its own header admits its lineage: it <em>“steals willy-nilly from the NextGen class loader”</em> — NextGen being Rice University’s 1998-onward project (Cartwright &amp; Steele, in fact!) that first ran reified generics for Java by exactly this template-expansion technique. More on that in §9.</p>

<p>On each request it sniffs the name (§3) and takes one of two routes:</p>

<p><strong>Route A — synthesis from nothing.</strong> For structural types — <code>Arrow</code>, <code>AbstractArrow</code>, <code>Tuple</code>, <code>ConcreteTuple</code>, <code>AnyTuple</code>, <code>Union</code>, closures (✉), and the RTTI class/interface pairs — there is no template anywhere. The name alone is the specification, and the loader emits the whole class with raw ASM calls. The subtle one is <code>instantiateUnion</code>: to build <code>Union⟦A,B⟧</code> it reflectively loads A and B <em>during the load of the union</em>, intersects their transitive interface sets, and emits a type implementing the common ones — Chase’s TypeMapping paper (§7 below) explains why unions must otherwise erase to <code>Object</code>.</p>

<p><strong>Route B — template expansion.</strong> For user generics: <code>InstantiationMap.canonicalizeStaticParameters</code> strips <code>List⟦ZZ32⟧</code> to the stem <code>List⟦⟧</code>, finds the template class and its <code>.xlation</code> sidecar in the component jar (both jars <em>and</em> their contents sit on the classpath via <code>bin/run_classpath</code>, so <code>getResourceAsStream</code> finds them), zips the formal parameter names from the sidecar against the actual arguments parsed from the request, and produces a substitution map. Then <code>ClassReader</code> feeds the template through <code>Instantiater</code> (rewrites the class name, supertypes, interfaces; synthesizes tuple-flattening bridge methods that don’t exist in the template) and <code>MethodInstantiater</code> (rewrites every single instruction — type mentions in <code>NEW</code>/<code>CHECKCAST</code>/field and method refs, symbolic RTTI references turned into chains of runtime factory calls, the ⎆ RPN nat-arithmetic evaluated, ✚/✖ stop markers honored), and finally out through — again — <code>ManglingClassWriter</code> into <code>defineClass</code>.</p>

<div class="callout amber">
<span class="callout-label">The papers explain the “why”</span>
<p>Why templates for generic <em>methods</em> too, compiled into separate top-level classes? MethodMapping.tex gives the one-sentence reason that unlocks the whole design: <em>“the JVM does not allow new methods to be added to an already-loaded class.”</em> A generic method instantiated at a new type needs new code, and its enclosing class is already loaded — so every generic method body becomes its own template class (tagged ☝), fetched through a per-call-site lookup-and-cache (hashcode + signature string → closure object). Every strange artifact in the bytecode follows from working around that single JVM constraint in 2011 — three years before <code>invokedynamic</code> matured and a decade before <code>defineHiddenClass</code>.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="optimizer"><span class="secno">§6</span>The optimizer that never ran</h2>
<p class="cite">ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/asmbytecodeoptimizer/ (30 files)</p>

<p><span class="verdict dead">Dead code</span> The answer to “do I need an explainer for <code>asmbytecodeoptimizer</code>” is: no, and here is the tombstone. The package is <strong>never invoked by the compiler</strong>. Its entry class <code>ByteCodeOptimizer</code> is package-private with its own <code>main</code>, reachable only through standalone shell scripts (<code>bin/BytecodeOptimize</code> and friends). The single cross-package reference from live code is <code>CodeGenMethodVisitor.java:120</code> borrowing its <code>opcNames[]</code> array to print opcode names in debug output.</p>

<p>What’s in it is a sketch, not a tool: a pass that <em>injects</em> a println marker (labelled a test, effectively), a peephole pass removing literal coercions, label indexing, a 395-line inliner hardwired to <code>CompilerBuiltin</code>/<code>CompilerLibrary</code> methods, and an abstract-interpretation framework whose instruction coverage is 86 “not yet implemented” stubs. The one pass with a real optimization idea — <code>GenerateUnboxedVersions</code> — is both commented out <em>and</em> unimplemented (it only prints). This was the beginning of an optimization story, abandoned when the project ended.</p>

<div class="callout">
<span class="callout-label">One live trap</span>
<p>The package contains its own <code>Opcodes.java</code>. Any file in the package that <code>import org.objectweb.asm.Opcodes</code> silently gets the local class instead — same-package resolution beats imports. The constant values happen to coincide today, which is exactly the kind of coincidence that bites during an upgrade. Worth remembering if we ever revive or delete the package (deletion, like BCEL, is the likely right answer — but that’s a decision for Pavol, not a default).</p>
</div>

<h2 id="docs"><span class="secno">§7</span>Where Sun wrote it down</h2>

<p>Your question — <em>“is this part of the runtime, the language spec, the .tex files, or something we reverse-engineer?”</em> — has a clean answer, and it’s good news.</p>

<p><strong>The Specification is silent.</strong> All ~150 spec .tex files (both <code>Specification/</code> and the 1.0-frozen copy) define the <em>language</em>; a scout grepped them for every implementation concept in this report and found zero content (the single textual hit is a LaTeX pun). The spec was never meant to describe the JVM mapping, and it doesn’t.</p>

<p><strong>The real documentation is in <code>Papers/</code>, in-repo, and excellent:</strong></p>

<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>Document</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>Papers/Implementation/MethodMapping.tex</code><br>Chase &amp; Naden, ©2010–11, 438 lines</td><td><em>“Fortress function and method encodings”</em> — the missing manual. Closures, generic functions, functional-method trampolines (☞), generic methods as separate template classes, the lookup/cache calling convention, overloaded dispatch. §3–§5 of this report are its subject matter, verified against 2026 source.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Papers/Implementation/TypeMapping.tex</code><br>Chase, 244 lines</td><td><em>“Mapping Fortress type relationships onto the JVM type system”</em> — traits→interfaces, objects→final classes, why Tuple/Arrow variance can’t pre-encode into the JVM hierarchy (element-wise checks instead), union types erased to <code>Object</code> (<code>Naming.ERASED_UNION_TYPE</code>), canonicalization, and a prescient section on interface injection — the feature they needed and the JVM never shipped.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Papers/Implementation/FortressEncodings-rendered.pdf</code></td><td>Pre-rendered combination of both, current as of the final 2012 commit. The LaTeX needs XeLaTeX plus fonts we may not have (Apple Symbols, Code2000, Zapf Dingbats — of course the naming-scheme paper needs exotic fonts); the PDF spares us.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Papers/Dispatch/</code></td><td>The POPL 2011 submission (Steele, Chase, Allen, Luchangco, Ryu) on multimethod dispatch via source-to-source rewriting — the theory behind the overload machinery.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Papers/RuntimeInstantiation/</code></td><td>Naden’s five dated 2012 working notes + <code>RTRinstantionTheory.tex</code>: enforcing the Return Type Rule at runtime. This is the exact frontier where the team was standing when funding ended (§9).</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>So: <strong>not reverse-engineering — verification.</strong> The papers describe intent circa 2011; the source is the ground truth of 2012; our job when touching this machinery is diffing the two. Everything in this report was cross-checked that way.</p>

<h2 id="saywhat"><span class="secno">§8</span><code>sayWhat</code> and the two execution paths</h2>

<p><em>“What does sayWhat mean? Is the compiler incomplete? Is complete Fortress only executable in the interpreter?”</em></p>

<h3>The word itself</h3>

<p><code>sayWhat</code> is the Fortress compiler’s way of saying “…what?” — the method CodeGen calls when it meets an AST node it cannot compile. It returns (call sites <code>throw</code> it) a <code>CompilerError</code>: “Can’t compile <em>x</em>”. And it comes with the most sympathetic four lines in the repo:</p>

<pre><code>private void allSayWhats() {
    return; // This is a great place for a breakpoint!
}</code></pre>
<p class="cite">CodeGen.java:1546 — an empty method that exists so one debugger breakpoint catches every place the compiler gives up</p>

<p>The inventory: 29 explicit <code>throw sayWhat(...)</code> sites, plus the catch-all — <code>defaultCase</code> at CodeGen.java:1668. CodeGen explicitly handles about <strong>47 of the 1,006 AST node kinds</strong>; everything unhandled falls through to <code>sayWhat</code>. Most of the 950 are unreachable (abstract nodes, or forms desugared away before codegen), but the reachable gaps are exactly the features ordinary programs use. The honest unsupported list, reconstructed from the throw sites and the <code>canCompile</code> gates: <strong>mutable top-level bindings</strong> (the single most user-visible failure — one <code>var</code> at top level sinks the compile), local (nested) functions and references to them, tupled and multi-variable bindings, <code>where</code> clauses, contracts (<code>requires</code>/<code>ensures</code>/<code>invariant</code>), and <code>forbid</code> clauses. Two <code>sayWhat</code> calls (CodeGen.java:4338, 4344) even forget to <code>throw</code> — they construct the error and drop it on the floor, a genuine latent bug.</p>

<h3>The two paths — and the surprise</h3>

<p>Both paths share one front end — same parser, same disambiguation, same phase driver (<code>PhaseOrder.java:125–147</code>). Then:</p>

<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Interpreter (“walk”)</th><th>Compiler (“compile” + “run”)</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Invocation</strong></td><td><code>fortress file.fss</code> — the default</td><td><code>fortress compile</code>, then <code>fortress run</code> (which bypasses Shell entirely — <code>bin/run</code> → <code>MainWrapper</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Execution</strong></td><td>Tree-walker: <code>Evaluator extends NodeAbstractVisitor&lt;FValue&gt;</code>, <code>eval(e) = e.accept(this)</code>. No bytecode, ever.</td><td>Everything in §2–§5 of this report</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Type checking</strong></td><td><strong>Never runs.</strong> TYPECHECK sits in the phase list, but <code>walk</code> never sets the flag (default <code>false</code>), and there is no flag to turn it on</td><td>Scala type checker, on (<code>setTypeChecking(true)</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Standard library</strong></td><td><code>FortressLibrary</code> — the big one</td><td><code>CompilerLibrary</code> — a much smaller subset (why <code>MOD</code> was “not defined” in NOTES.md’s tests)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Completeness</strong></td><td>4 not-implemented sites (array comprehensions, keyword args…)</td><td>29 + the gaps above</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Test suite</strong></td><td><code>ant testSystem</code> — 382 tests</td><td><code>ant testFast</code> — ~1,400 tests</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>Read that type-checking row again, because it’s the fact that reframes everything: <strong>the interpreter is a dynamically-typed Fortress.</strong> Fortress’s celebrated static type system — arguably the reason the language exists — runs only on the compiler path, the one that can’t yet compile all programs. A program that “works in the interpreter” was never type-checked at all.</p>

<div class="callout">
<span class="callout-label">History, run backwards</span>
<p>NOTES.md (pluckyporcupine’s era) concluded: <em>“I’m beginning to suspect that the compiler was being phased out in favor of the walk command.”</em> The git history says the exact opposite. A 2009 commit message already speaks of “when we were working primarily on the interpreter” in the past tense; the entire final year of commits (Jan–Aug 2012) is compiler/RTTI/overloading work; the interpreter-oriented code in NamingCzar is annotated “I think this is old interpreter-centric code.” The interpreter was the reference prototype. The compiler was the future. It didn’t die of obsolescence — it ran out of runway, mid-construction, when Oracle ended the project in 2012. <code>sayWhat</code> marks not decay but the exact waterline where construction stopped.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="questions"><span class="secno">§9</span>Your questions, answered</h2>

<div class="qa">
<p class="q">“Fortress was breaking ice in uncharted territory, basically the cutting edge of type research? I’m out of my depth… was there enough published research in your pretraining to ground us?”</p>
<p>Mostly, no ice-breaking — and yes, plenty of grounding. Deep breath: roughly 90% of what this report describes was <em>known engineering</em> by the mid-2000s, with a literature I know well:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>NextGen</strong> (Rice University, Cartwright &amp; <em>Steele</em>, 1998→) ran reified generics for Java via classloader template expansion — the direct ancestor; Fortress’s loader says in its own comments that it steals from NextGen’s.</li>
<li><strong>.NET CLR generics</strong> (Kennedy &amp; Syme, PLDI 2001) is the fully-worked, production-proven design for runtime instantiation of reified generics — same problem, richer solution, extensively published.</li>
<li>The name mangling is John Rose’s published <strong>“symbolic freedom”</strong> encoding from the invokedynamic work.</li>
<li>Multimethod dispatch and overload resolution: a literature going back through Cecil, Dylan, and CLOS, plus Fortress’s own POPL submission in <code>Papers/Dispatch/</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The genuinely-stuck 10% has a name and an address: enforcing the <strong>Return Type Rule at runtime</strong> — when dynamic dispatch on a generic method picks an implementation, guaranteeing its return type still fits what the call site was promised requires solving type constraints at instantiation time. That is what <code>Papers/RuntimeInstantiation/</code> is wrestling with in notes dated months before the shutdown, and it’s the part Steele himself flagged at JuliaCon 2016: <em>“So we had a grand vision, but could not quite pull it off.”</em> We should treat that corner with respect — but we don’t need to solve it to keep the ladder climbing, fix the caches, or even extend the library. It only bites deep in the generic-dispatch machinery.</p>
</div>

<div class="qa">
<p class="q">“Isn’t this just because Fortress implemented generics before they were supported in Java per se? Is this the main complexity source, and could we build on later generics advances?”</p>
<p>Half right, and the precision matters. Java <em>had</em> generics when this was built (since 2004) — but <strong>erased</strong> generics: at runtime, <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> are the same class, types scrubbed away. Fortress needed <strong>reified</strong> generics — each instantiation a real runtime type you can dispatch on, ask questions of, and dynamically instantiate — because Fortress semantics (multimethod dispatch on generic types, runtime type inference at call sites) demand it. Java still doesn’t have that in 2026 (Project Valhalla has been circling it for a decade), so there is no “later Java generics advance” to build on — the template machinery earns its keep.</p>
<p>So yes: reification-on-an-erased-VM is the main complexity source — that, plus three Fortress-specific amplifiers: tuple/arrow <em>variance</em> (covariant returns, contravariant arguments — can’t pre-encode in Java’s hierarchy), <em>union/intersection types</em> from inference (combinatorially impossible to predeclare), and the Return Type Rule above. What later JVMs <em>do</em> offer is better machinery for the same design: <code>invokedynamic</code> + <code>MethodHandles</code> could replace the hand-rolled lookup/cache convention, and <code>defineHiddenClass</code> (JDK 15) is practically purpose-built for “loader conjures a class from thin air.” Those are modernization options for goal 4, not rescues we’re missing.</p>
</div>

<div class="qa">
<p class="q">“Can researching Scala or Clojure implementations help? Maybe they picked up enough of Fortress’s ideas after it was abandoned…”</p>
<p>Less than you’d hope, for a structural reason: both went the <em>other</em> way on the central question. Scala and Clojure accept JVM erasure and live with it; Fortress refused it and built reification by hand. Scala’s <code>@specialized</code> and the miniboxing research are the nearest Scala-side literature (compile-time stamping of type-specialized variants — a static cousin of Fortress’s runtime templates), worth reading for the trade-off analysis but not a parts bin. Clojure contributes essentially nothing here — dynamically typed, erasure-friendly, protocol dispatch. The true relatives to study are the CLR (reified generics done right, with money and a VM they controlled), NextGen (same trick, same era, same Steele), and — poignantly — <strong>Julia</strong>, which Steele’s 2016 talk all but names as the heir: multiple dispatch, runtime specialization, JIT-time instantiation. Fortress’s ideas didn’t flow into Scala; they flowed into Julia.</p>
</div>

<div class="qa">
<p class="q">“Is there any parallel with Scala’s REPL?”</p>
<p>A surface one only, and it runs opposite to the intuition. The Scala REPL is a <em>compiler</em> in a loop: every line you type is wrapped in a synthetic object, compiled to real bytecode, and handed to a classloader. The Fortress interpreter is the opposite — a tree-walker that never generates a byte of bytecode. So the REPL is actually a (weak) parallel to Fortress’s <em>compiled</em> path — “generate bytecode, load it through a custom classloader on demand” — while the interpreter has no Scala analogue. If you want a same-species comparison for the interpreter: it’s a classic AST-walking evaluator, the kind you’d meet in SICP or early Ruby, wired to a work-stealing ForkJoin pool because even the prototype had to demonstrate implicit parallelism.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="reading"><span class="secno">§10</span>Reading order, if you want to go deeper</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong><code>Papers/Implementation/FortressEncodings-rendered.pdf</code></strong> — the Chase/Naden manual, side by side with…</li>
<li><strong><code>InstantiatingClassloader.java</code></strong> — read <code>loadClass</code> top-down; every branch corresponds to a section of the PDF.</li>
<li><strong><code>Naming.java</code></strong> — the tag-character glossary is executable documentation; the constants have explanatory comments.</li>
<li>Set <code>-Dfortress.bytecodes.expanded.directory=/tmp/expanded</code>, run a compiled program, and <code>javap -c</code> the conjured classes — the whole §2 pipeline, made tangible.</li>
<li><strong><code>Papers/RuntimeInstantiation/</code></strong> — only when you want to stand at the exact spot where they stopped.</li>
</ol>

<hr>

<p style="color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.9rem;">Compiled from three source-reading expeditions across the runtime system, the codegen package, the papers, the spec, and the git history; all file:line citations verified against tip <code>c4c90f9</code>. Companion to “The Probe and the Pin.”</p>

</div>

